      <div class="eyebrow">RF / EM &nbsp;·&nbsp; Antenna design</div>
      <h1><span class="chrome-text">We almost didn't patent this one</span></h1>
      <p class="post-subtitle">Notes on my stacked center-slotted patch</p>
      <div class="post-byline"><span><strong>Dom Belgiovane, PhD</strong></span><span>Tuesday, November 3, 2026</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="post container-narrow patent">
    <figure class="wide hero" style="max-width:1280px;margin-top:0">
      <div class="frame"><picture><source srcset="/blog/009-stacked-patch-patent/images/hero.webp" type="image/webp"><img src="/blog/009-stacked-patch-patent/images/hero.jpg" width="1280" height="720" alt="Illustrative EMerge model of the stacked center-slotted patch, with simulated L1 surface current on the upper patch"></picture></div>
      <figcaption>Illustrative simulation in the open-source EMerge solver, enhanced for display. Dimensions are assumed.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="measure">
      <p>I didn't really think this antenna was patentable. It is a simple design: two stacked patches, a slot in the middle of each, and one probe. I kind of took it for granted at first. My co-inventor, Chad Wangsvick, was the one who pushed me to file. He thought it was novel, and my reaction was, "Really, you think so?"</p>
      <p>So we did more research and looked at what else was out there, and there was nothing quite like it. On review, we realized it had enough novelty to patent. That became US 12,562,485 B2, "Probe-fed circularly-polarized stacked center-slotted patch antenna," assigned to Raytheon Company, filed March 22, 2023 (Appl. 18/187,769), published as US 2024/0322433 A1, and granted February 24, 2026.</p>
      <p>No single feature is what made it patentable. It was the combination:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>One probe feed</strong> for both patches</li>
        <li><strong>Circular polarization</strong> from that single feed</li>
        <li><strong>Two bands</strong>, GPS L1 and L2</li>
        <li><strong>A compact footprint</strong></li>
      </ul>
      <p>On top of that, the elements can sit close together. That matters for controlled reception pattern antenna (CRPA) arrays, where anti-jamming and null-steering algorithms need elements that behave alike in gain and phase.</p>
      <p>The usual single-feed CP trick is to clip two opposite corners of the patch. The patent's background notes that corner-clipped patches have narrow bandwidth and high inter-element coupling. A clipped corner changes the fields right at the patch edge, and the edge is where neighboring elements talk to each other. Our design moves that job to the middle of the patch.</p>

      <h2>How it works: two stacked patches, one probe</h2>
      <p>The example embodiment is a dual-band GPS antenna covering L1 (1.57542 GHz) and L2 (1.2276 GHz). From the bottom up:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A metal ground plane, 4 in x 4 in in the example</li>
        <li>A first substrate, 2 in x 2 in x 0.2 in, with the lower patch on top (the L2 patch)</li>
        <li>A second substrate of the same size, with the upper patch on top (the L1 patch)</li>
        <li>A single coaxial feed: outer conductor to the ground plane, center pin soldered to the upper patch</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The example substrate is Rogers TMM 10i, a ceramic thermoset laminate. The patent treats all of these dimensions as one example, not a limit.</p>
      <p>The renders and plots in this post come from a quick illustrative model I put together in the open-source EMerge solver. The patent does not give patch dimensions, so the model uses assumed values, and the antenna was not built or measured.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="wide" style="max-width:1400px">
      <div class="frame"><picture><source srcset="/blog/009-stacked-patch-patent/images/fig1a-stacked-patch-top.webp" type="image/webp"><img src="/blog/009-stacked-patch-patent/images/fig1a-stacked-patch-top.png" width="1400" height="1053" alt="Fig. 1A: the full stack, with the upper (L1) patch, its diagonal slot 111, the feed via pin 113, and two layers of TMM 10i on a 4 in ground plane. Source: US 12,562,485 B2, Fig. 1A."></picture></div>
      <figcaption>Fig. 1A: the full stack, with the upper (L1) patch, its diagonal slot 111, the feed via pin 113, and two layers of TMM 10i on a 4 in ground plane. Source: US 12,562,485 B2, Fig. 1A.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="measure">
      <p>The center pin passes through the lower patch without touching it. A void in the lower patch around the pin lets RF couple capacitively into it. So one probe drives the upper patch directly and the lower patch by coupling. The patent notes that the same idea extends to three or more patches: direct connection to the top-most patch and capacitive coupling to the rest.</p>
    </div>

