    <div class="measure">
      <h2>Why a center slot</h2>
      <p>Each patch has a diagonal, non-radiating slot through its center, at roughly 45 degrees to the patch sides. The slot delays the current along one diagonal relative to the other, which excites two orthogonally polarized fields with a phase difference between them. That is the same job the clipped corners do, moved to the middle of the patch.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="wide" style="max-width:1400px">
      <div class="frame"><picture><source srcset="/blog/009-stacked-patch-patent/images/emerge/current_l1.webp" type="image/webp"><img src="/blog/009-stacked-patch-patent/images/emerge/current_l1.jpg" width="1400" height="1243" alt="Surface current on the upper (L1) patch at 1.575 GHz, crowding around the ends of the center slot while the patch edges stay comparatively clean. Illustrative simulation in the open-source EMerge solver. Dimensions are assumed; the patent does not specify them. This is not a measured result or the patented design's exact dimensions."></picture></div>
      <figcaption>Surface current on the upper (L1) patch at 1.575 GHz, crowding around the ends of the center slot while the patch edges stay comparatively clean. Illustrative simulation in the open-source EMerge solver. Dimensions are assumed; the patent does not specify them. This is not a measured result or the patented design's exact dimensions.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="measure">
      <p>This is what lets the elements sit close together. In the patent's words, the center slot is a tuning feature that does not perturb the fields at the edge. With clean edges, the CP is less disturbed by neighbors than with a corner-clipped or rectangular CP stacked patch, and the patent ties that reduced coupling to better gain and phase flatness, especially at the waterline.</p>
      <p>Slots in the same orientation on both patches give RHCP; opposite orientations give LHCP. Because the slots do not radiate, each one can be rotated on its own to clean up CP in its band.</p>

      <h2>The "diving board" offset</h2>
      <p>The upper patch does not sit centered over the lower one. It is shifted so that it overlaps and overhangs, in what the patent calls a "diving board" configuration. That offset helps match the upper band. Together with the size of the coupling void around the pin, it lets each band be matched without separate matching circuits or extra layers, which is a big part of why the single probe and the compact footprint work together. The independent claims include this offset.</p>
    </div>

